\wg21_head(
  title = {\tt{errno}-less math}
){
\dl{
  \dt{Document number:} \dd{\docnum{D4447R0}}
  \dt{Date:}            \dd{\tt{2026-11-10}}
  \dt{Audience:}        \dd{LEWG}
  \dt{Project:}         \dd{ISO/IEC 14882 Programming Languages — C++, ISO/IEC JTC1/SC22/WG21}
  \dt{Reply-to:}        \dd{Jan Schultke <\mail{janschultke@gmail.com}>\br
                            Matt Borland <\mail{matt@mattborland.com}>}
  \dt{GitHub Issue:}    \dd{\ref("https://wg21.link/P3688/github")}
  \dt{Source:}          \dd{\ref("https://github.com/eisenwave/cpp-proposals/blob/main/src/template-9.cow")}
}
\hr
}

\Babstract{
We propose the ability to disable interactions with \tt{errno} in mathematical functions.
}

\h2(listed=false){Contents}

\make_contents

\h2{Introduction}

Functions in header \header{cmath} may currently write to \tt{errno} to indicate errors such
as domain errors, overflow, or underflow.
For example, \tcode{std::sqrt(-1)} doesn't simply yield NaN;
it also sets \tt{errno} to \tt{EDOM}.

\tcode{errno} is the cockroach of the C programming language.
It gets everywhere,
is uniformly disliked,
but it is shockingly resilient against efforts to remove it.

With modern language design and hardware capabilities in mind,
\tcode{errno} is obsolete and harmful to performance.
\tcode{errno} is legacy baggage inherited from Unix in the 1970s
that ended up standardized in C89.
However, even at that time, \tcode{errno} was widely understood to be a mistake.
In 1990, the WG14 convener P.J. Plauger wrote the following in \ref("Plauger1990"):

\Bquote{
If I had to identify one part of the C Standard that is uniformly disliked,
I would not have to look far.
Nobody likes \i{errno} or the machinery that it implies.
I can't recall anybody defending this approach to error reporting,
not in two dozen or more meetings of X3J11.
Several alternatives were proposed over the years.
At least one faction favored simply discarding \i{errno}.
Yet it endures.
}

\h3{Performance problem}

The runtime checks and write to \tcode{errno}
can worsen the performance of mathematical operations
and disable several optimizations because mathematical functions are not pure.
\tcode{std::sqrt} is an extreme example
because the entire operation can be performed within a few CPU cycles
(approx. 20 cycles latency, 6 cycles throughput)
and using a single CPU instruction:

\style{
  .center {
    margin-left: auto;
    margin-right: auto;
  }

  .tony-table td {
    background-color: var(--deep-background-color);
    width: 50%;
  }
}

\table(class="center"){
  \tr{
    \th{Architecture}
    \th{Instruction}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{x86_64}
    \td{SQRTSD}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{ARM}
    \td{FSQRT}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{RISC-V}
    \td{FSQRT.S}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{WASM}
    \td{f64.sqrt}
  }
}

\cowel_macro("fno_math_errno"){\ref("https://gcc.gnu.org/onlinedocs/gcc/Optimize-Options.html#index-fno-math-errno"){\tt{-fno-math-errno}}}

Due to this problem,
some compilers provide a flag to disable writing to \tcode{errno} in mathematical functions,
which is \fno_math_errno for GCC and Clang.

\Bex{
Consider the difference in output for the following functions:
\cppblock{
double s(double x) {
  return std::sqrt(x);
}
std::array<double, 4> vs(const std::array<double, 4>& x) {
  return { std::sqrt(x[0]), std::sqrt(x[1]),
           std::sqrt(x[2]), std::sqrt(x[3]) };
}
}

\table(class="center tony-table"){
  \tr{
    \th{\tt{clang++-24 -O3\br-march=x86-64-v3}}
    \th{\tt{clang++-24 -O3\br-march=x86-64-v3 -fno-math-errno}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){s(double):}
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      sqrt@PLT
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        ret
}}
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){s(double):}
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        ret
}}
  }
  \tr{
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){vs(std::array<double, 4ul> const&):}
        push    r14
        push    rbx
        push    rax
        mov     r14, rsi
        mov     rbx, rdi
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [rsi]
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_2
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 8]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jae     .LBB1_4
.LBB1_5:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        jmp     .LBB1_6
.LBB1_2:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 8]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_5
.LBB1_4:
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
.LBB1_6:
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 8], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 16]
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_8
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 16], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 24]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jae     .LBB1_10
.LBB1_11:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        jmp     .LBB1_12
.LBB1_8:
        call    sqrt@PLT
        vxorpd  xmm1, xmm1, xmm1
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 16], xmm0
        vmovsd  xmm0, qword ptr [r14 + 24]
        vucomisd        xmm0, xmm1
        jb      .LBB1_11
.LBB1_10:
        vsqrtsd xmm0, xmm0, xmm0
.LBB1_12:
        vmovsd  qword ptr [rbx + 24], xmm0
        mov     rax, rbx
        add     rsp, 8
        pop     rbx
        pop     r14
        ret
}}
    \td{\codeblock("asm", borders=false){
\cowel_highlight_as("name-label"){vs(std::array<double, 4ul> const&):}
        vsqrtpd ymm0, ymmword ptr [rsi]
        mov     rax, rdi
        vmovupd ymmword ptr [rdi], ymm0
        vzeroupper
        ret
}}
  }
}
Note that while the difference in code size is quite dramatic,
the length of the critical path is not impacted as much.
In both the scalar and vector case,
there is a \q{happy path} (i.e. non-NaN) which is checked for,
and \code("asm"){call sqrt} is only a fallback solution.
}

\Bex{
While this optimization is not performed by compilers in practice,
it is theoretically possible to transform \tcode{std::ldexp(x, 3)} into \tcode{x * 8}
(and other calls to \tcode{std::ldexp} with constant exponents)
because both expressions are an equivalent multiplication by \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mn{3}}};
the only difference is that \tcode{std::ldexp}
sets \tcode{errno} to \tcode{ERANGE} on overflow.

Such an optimization could have dramatic benefits in the vectorized case
because it turns several calls to \tcode{std::ldexp} into a single vector multiplication.
}

However, crucially, \fno_math_errno is a non-conforming and non-portable optimization,
as described in the GCC docs:

\Bquote{
\b{\tt{-fno-math-errno}}
\Bindent{\cowel_paragraphs{
Do not set \tt{errno} after calling math functions that are executed with a single instruction,
e.g., \tcode{sqrt}.
A program that relies on IEEE exceptions for math error handling
may want to use this flag for speed while maintaining IEEE arithmetic compatibility.

This option is not turned on by any \tt{-O} option besides \tt{-Ofast}
since it can result in incorrect output for programs
that depend on an exact implementation of IEEE or ISO rules/specifications for math functions.
It may, however, yield faster code for programs
that do not require the guarantees of these specifications.

The default is \tt{-fmath-errno}.

On Darwin systems, the math library never sets \tcode{errno}.
There is therefore no reason for the compiler to consider the possibility that it might,
and \tt{-fno-math-errno} is the default.
}}
}

\h3{[simd] problem}

As shown in the example of \tcode{vs()} above,
whether \tcode{errno} is accessed impacts code generation dramatically.
Dozens of instructions can be effectively turned into a single \code("asm"){vsqrtpd} instruction
by just ignoring \tcode{errno}.
\header{simd} was designed with this problem in mind,
and throughout subclause \eelis{simd.math} and \eelis{simd.complex.math},
\fno_math_errno is permitted for \tcode{std::simd} functions:

\Bquote{
\remarks
It is unspecified whether errno\iref{errno} is accessed.
}

However, this is only a partial solution to the problem.
In reality, any high-quality SIMD library is going to need \tcode{errno}-less variants
of math functions.
The whole point of \header{simd} is to provide high-performance vectorized operations,
which is antithetical to the overhead of setting \tcode{errno}.
If a C++ implementation provides such function,
an \tcode{errno}-less \header{cmath} can be implemented in terms of \header{simd}.

\Bex{
\tcode{std::sqrt} can be implemented using \tcode{std::simd::sqrt} as follows:
\cppblock{
constexpr \exposid{floating-point-type} sqrt(\exposid{floating-point-type} x) {
  return simd::sqrt(simd::vec<\exposid{floating-point-type}, 1>(x))[0];
}
}
}

If an implementation provided \tcode{errno}-less functions for \tcode{std::simd},
it would be silly if users had to port all their existing \tcode{std::\exposid{function}} calls
to \tcode{std::simd::\exposid{function}} calls.
They should simply have the upgrade option to make all their existing functions \tcode{errno}-less too.

\h3{\tcode{constexpr} problem}

Since there can be no writes to \tcode{errno} during constant evaluation,
the committee is stuck with two bad options
regarding the use of math functions in constant expressions:
\ul{
  \li{
    Allow \tcode{std::sqrt(-1)} to be a constant expression
    and \q{silently} ignore \tcode{errno},
    even if the same expression evaluated at runtime would have modified \tcode{errno}.
  }
  \li{
    Disallow \tcode{std::sqrt(-1)} during constant evaluation.
    This is the status quo,
    and prohibits many uses of math functions.
    For example, \tcode{std::ldexp(1, x)} cannot be used
    to check whether \math{\msup{\mn{2}\mi{x}}} is representable as \tcode{double}
    (e.g. inside \tcode{if constexpr})
    because the answer is either \q{yes} or \q{ill-formed};
    at runtime, one can check whether the result is infinity.
  }
}

Optimization flags like \fno_math_errno cannot fix this design problem either.

However, if the standard provided a \q{mode} where math functions do not write to \tcode{errno}
(neither at runtime nor during constant evaluation),
then math functions could always be used in constant expressions
(at least for ISO/IEC 60559 floating-point types)
and would do the same as at runtime
(except for slight precision/rounding differences).
This would still present a portability concern
in the sense that some code would only compile in \q{errno-less mode},
but that is arguably an improvement over the current situation.

\h2{Design}

\h3{Proposed solution}

To address the problems described above,
we propose an \q{errno-less mode} for math functions.
This essentially requires the compiler and standard library to provide a flag
which deactivates writes to \tcode{errno} for math functions.

One major problem with this idea is that on platforms where writing to \tcode{errno} is enabled,
the implementation needs to provide a second copy of \tt{libc} or at least \tt{libm}
that has been compiled in \q{errno-less mode}.
Every single math function would need to have an \q{errno-less} variant available.
On platforms where \q{errno-less mode} is already the default (e.g. Darwin),
no action is required.

\Bnote{
The aforementioned \ref("#constexpr-problem") is not resolved in this paper,
but provides motivation for the direction.
}

\h3{Floating-point exceptions}

In addition to \tcode{errno} as a mechanism for reporting errors,
\header{cmath} functions may also produce floating-point exceptions.

In fact, a hosted implementation is required by the C standard to use
\tcode{errno} or floating-point exceptions (or both) to report errors.
This requirement needs to be lifted because floating-point exceptions
simply don't exist in some environments, such as on WASM.
However, it should still be possible to provide a hosted C++ implementation on WASM,
with \tt{libc} functions delegated to the
\ref("https://wasi.dev/"){WASI (WebAssembly System Interface)}.

Furthermore, we don't propose an \q{exception-less mode}
in addition to a a \tcode{errno}-less mode because
\ul{
  \li{
    this means that on implementations that already don't write to \tcode{errno},
    they certainly need only one math library,
  }
  \li{
    the entirety of the floating-point environment is already an optional feature, and
  }
  \li{
    floating-point exceptions are less problematic from a performance standpoint
    and are simply byproducts of hardware instructions.
  }
}

\h3{Feature testing}

Feature testing should work entirely through \tcode{math_errhandling},
which is a standard library bitmask macro,
where \tcode{(math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO) != 0} is \tcode{true}
if \tcode{errno} is used for error handling in \header{cmath}.

Our proposal simply mandates that an implementation must have a compiler flag
to make that expression \tcode{false},
i.e. to disable \tcode{errno}.

\h3{Is this not merely quality of implementation?}

One may argue that the change in this paper is somewhat pointless
because providing an \tcode{errno}-less math library is already permitted.
At first glance, it seems like we are mandating quality of implementation.
However, there are key factors that make standardization necessary:
\ul{
  \li{
    An \tcode{errno}-less math mode is likely necessary to resolve the \ref("#constexpr-problem").
  }
  \li{
    Hosted implementations \em{must} provide floating-point exceptions
    or \tcode{errno} for error handling currently,
    which forces implementations into writing to \tcode{errno} on platforms like WASM.
    We see this as an unnecessary and arbitrary constraint.
  }
  \li{
    A new C++ \tcode{std::math} library
    presumably should't be interacting with \tcode{errno}, ever.
    \tcode{errno} is widely considered a historical mistake,
    especially for math functions.
    This makes our proposal a hard requirement for proposals such as
    \ref("P4188R1") \q{Extensible Math Functions for C++}.
    A customization point like \tcode{std::math::sqrt}
    still needs some underlying function to do the computation for e.g. \tcode{float},
    and if \tcode{std::sqrt} doesn't write to \tcode{errno},
    that makes it a suitable candidate.
  }
}

\h2{Implementation experience}

Providing an \tt{errno}-less mode
requires little to no changes to the source code of existing math libraries,
since they are often configurable already.
Math libraries fall into three categories:
\ul{
  \li{
    Libraries that never write to \tcode{errno} anyway.
    These already comply with \tcode{errno}-less mode.
  }
  \li{
    Libraries that unconditionally write to \tcode{errno}.
    These can be retrofitted so that their \tcode{__set_errno} (example taken from glibc)
    function is turned into a \tcode{__math_set_errno} or similar,
    where \tcode{__math_set_errno} would do nothing in \tt{errno}-less mode.
  }
  \li{
    Libraries that already support being compiled in either mode,
    such as LLVM's \tt{libc}.
  }
}

\Bex{
LLVM's \tt{libc} has the following helper function in
\ref("https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/libc/src/__support/FPUtil/FEnvImpl.h"){\tt{libc/src/__support/FPUtil/FEnvImpl.h}}:
\cppblock{
\cowel_highlight_as("name-macro"){LIBC_INLINE LIBC_CONSTEXPR_DEFAULT} void
set_errno_if_required([[maybe_unused]] int err) {
  if (!cpp::is_constant_evaluated()) {
#ifndef LIBC_MATH_HAS_NO_ERRNO
    if (math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO)
      libc_errno = err;
#endif // LIBC_MATH_HAS_NO_ERRNO
  }
}
}
}

In essence, our paper requires no or only trivial code changes.
The difficult part is deciding on what \q{secondary math library} to ship
and whether to integrate it into the C++ runtime library.

\h2{Wording}

The changes are relative to \ref("N5054").

\h3(show_number=false){[intro.compliance.general]}

Immediately following \eelis{intro.compliance.general#8}
(i.e. following the definition of \q{hardened implementation}),
insert a new paragraph:

\Bins{
An implementation shall provide a configuration option so that
\tcode{math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO} equals \tcode{0}\iref{cmath.syn}.
}

\h3(show_number=false){[cmath.syn]}

Append a new paragraph to \eelis{cmath.syn}:

\Bins{
The meaning of the macro \tcode{math_errhandling} is the same
as of that in the C standard library header \header{math.h},
except that\ul{
  \li{
    \tcode{math_errhandling} may equal \tcode{0}
    even for a hosted implementation, and
  }
  \li{
    if \tcode{math_errhandling & MATH_ERRNO} equals \tcode{0},
    \tcode{errno} is not accessed by any functions described in subclause \eelis{c.math}.
  }
}
}

